# Line endings.
#
# The dev machine has core.autocrlf=true, so without these rules a fresh
# Windows clone checks shell scripts out with CRLF. A CRLF in a shebang
# makes the Linux kernel look for an interpreter literally named
# "/bin/sh\r" and fail with "no such file or directory" — an error that
# points at the file rather than at the line ending, and so costs an hour.
#
# This repo promises that a fresh clone works (./bootstrap.ps1 -> just ->
# test-unit), and gui_entrypoint.sh is COPYed into the container image and
# executed there. That promise is only true if these stay LF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Not executed as a script, but keeping it LF stops CRLF leaking into RUN
# commands that get baked into the image.
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Read by `just`, whose recipe bodies are handed to bash.
justfile text eol=lf

# The art generators (D-064). `art/build.py` writes generated/manifest.json's
# source_hash by hashing these files' BYTES, and test_art_assets.gd re-hashes
# the same bytes through Godot to prove generated/ is not stale. A CRLF
# substitution changes those bytes, so the two disagree and the test reports
# "generated/ is stale — run `just build-assets`" when nothing is stale at
# all. Rebuilding on a CRLF checkout would bake a CRLF hash in and move the
# failure elsewhere rather than fix it.
*.py text eol=lf

# Generated art (D-064). These are committed so a fresh clone plays without
# installing Blender, and they must never be touched by the line-ending
# machinery — the dev machine has core.autocrlf=true, and a CRLF substitution
# inside a .glb or a .png corrupts it silently rather than loudly.
*.glb binary
*.exr binary
*.png binary
